notes=['Recovered alternating parity-to-weak translation over infinite words and the state-count measure.','Defined the worst-case minimum output-state function and requested matching asymptotic bounds, using the user-authorized broadening from a lower-bound-improvement question to its underlying complexity.','Specified alphabet scope, unrestricted transition formulas, acceptance games, SCC weakness and lack of a construction-time requirement.','Separated word automata from tree automata and general translations from universal-tree or separating-automaton constructions.','Checked the latest source revision and the distinct 2023/2024 disambiguation result; removed all suggested lower-bound techniques.']
save(i,dict(
 title='State complexity of parity-to-weak word automata',
 formal='Determine, up to constant factors as n→∞, the worst-case number W(n) of states needed to represent an n-state alternating parity word automaton by an equivalent alternating weak word automaton. For each integer n≥2, let W(n)=sup_A min_B |Q_B|, where A ranges over all alternating parity automata with 1≤|Q_A|≤n over arbitrary nonempty finite alphabets, and B ranges over alternating weak automata over the same alphabet satisfying L(B)=L(A) on all infinite words. Priorities of A are unrestricted, and the alphabet may depend on A and n. This asks for matching upper and lower bounds on the minimum output-state count, not on the time needed to compute a translation.',
 definitions='An alternating parity word automaton is a tuple A=(Σ,Q,q₀,τ,π), with a nonempty finite input alphabet Σ, a nonempty finite state set Q, initial state q₀∈Q, transitions τ:Q×Σ→B⁺(Q), and a state-priority map π:Q→ℕ. B⁺(Q) consists of finite Boolean formulas made from state variables, conjunction, disjunction and the constants true and false, without negated state variables. Transition-formula length, alphabet size and the numerical encoding of priorities are not part of the state count. Each automaton and all its formulas are finite, but there is no common polynomial bound on their description length. An input is an infinite word w=w₀w₁…∈Σ^ω. In the acceptance game the current position is (q,t), initially (q₀,0). The existential player chooses a subset Y⊆Q satisfying τ(q,w_t), interpreted with exactly Y true; the universal player chooses a state r∈Y, giving position (r,t+1). A player unable to move loses, which defines the semantics of true, false and an empty chosen subset. If play continues forever, the existential player wins exactly when the largest priority occurring infinitely often is even. The word is accepted if the existential player has a winning strategy; L(A) is the set of accepted words. Both players may use the full finite play history; this is language acceptance on the given word, not a requirement to resolve nondeterminism without knowledge of the word. The transition graph has an edge q→r whenever r occurs in τ(q,a) for at least one a∈Σ. An alternating weak automaton has a priority map constant on each strongly connected component of this graph; equivalently, each component is wholly accepting or wholly rejecting, and an infinite play is accepted according to the parity of its eventual component. Priorities can therefore be replaced by a two-valued accepting/rejecting labeling without changing the language. No determinism or restriction to singleton components is required. Equivalent automata accept precisely the same infinite words over the same alphabet. W(n) maximizes over the full parity class, including automata whose number of distinct priorities grows with n. There are at most n distinct priorities; preserving their order and parity permits normalization to priorities at most 2n without changing the language. The source supplies a finite upper bound on the required output states independent of the alphabet, so every inner minimum exists and the supremum of these integer minima is finite and attained. Using at most n states also removes irrelevant padding choices. An asymptotic determination supplies an explicitly described positive function g and constants c,C>0,N such that c g(n)≤W(n)≤C g(n) for every integer n≥N. There is no demanded running time, uniform construction bound, approximation of the accepted language, or probability of error.',
 question_type='asymptotic_complexity',
 answer_criterion='Give a mathematical description of the growth of W(n), with a universal upper bound for every eligible input automaton and a matching lower bound against every equivalent alternating weak automaton. Match within constant multiplicative factors. A better isolated lower bound is progress but does not determine the full asymptotic target. A lower bound only for one translation scheme, for weak automata with additional restrictions, or for tree languages does not establish the required lower bound.',
 context_blocks=[dict(title='A gap between necessary and sufficient states',text='The source records an Ω(n log n) lower bound on the number of required weak states, already arising for Büchi input automata. Its general parity-to-weak construction has quasipolynomial size, leaving a wide gap. These are output-state counts, not a claim that the multiplicative blow-up ratio itself is Ω(n log n).',citation='primary'),dict(title='The number of priorities affects known upper bounds',text='Theorem 7 gives a polynomial bound when the number of priorities is O(log n), and a bound n^{O(log(d/log n))} when d grows faster than log n. W(n) takes the worst case over d, rather than limiting the input to logarithmically many priorities.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Editorial expansion of the source question',text='The concluding passage asks whether the Ω(n log n) lower bound can be improved. Under the user-authorized review, the main target is expanded to the underlying worst-case state complexity with matching bounds. This stronger quantitative target includes the original motivation but is not asserted to be equivalent to merely improving that lower bound.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Different automata transformations have different bounds',text='The 2018 paper separates the word setting from exponential succinctness gaps over trees. Later disambiguation work starts with a weak automaton and converts it to an unambiguous Büchi automaton; it does not give a tighter parity-to-weak state bound.',citation='disambiguation')],
 why='Measures the intrinsic cost of simplifying parity acceptance while retaining alternation, separating unavoidable representational complexity from the cost of particular constructions. Efficiently constructible improvements also have implications for parity-game algorithms, but construction time is not built into this state-count target.',
 progress=[dict(date='2018',text='Earlier work distinguishes the Ω(n log n) lower bound over words from exponential separations over trees.',citation='previous'),dict(date='2019',text='The universal-tree construction improves the general upper bound to quasipolynomial size and explicitly leaves the lower-bound gap open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2023',text='Disambiguation of alternating weak automata is improved, addressing a different source and target automaton class.',citation='disambiguation')],
 references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the source definitions, Theorem 7 and open-question passage; checked arXiv:1903.12620v2, revised 4 July 2019. Subsequent-work searches through 12 September 2026 found the 2023 disambiguation theorem, also published in Theoretical Computer Science in 2024, but no matching general parity-to-weak bound. Searches are not exhaustive, and no new lower-bound proof was attempted.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,
 review_note='The target is now the asymptotic worst-case minimum state count. Its expansion beyond the source’s request for any improved lower bound is explicit. Alphabet size and transition syntax are not silently included in n; tree-language and construction-specific bounds are not substituted.',
 importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A basic succinctness gap between expressive acceptance conditions over infinite words, with structural relevance to verification and parity games. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),
 working_summary=dict(sentences=['Alternating parity automata recognize infinite-word languages using a parity condition on recurring states.','An alternating weak automaton must label each strongly connected component consistently as accepting or rejecting.','The question now asks for the worst-case minimum number of weak states as a function of the number of parity states, up to constant factors.','Known lower bounds are Ω(n log n), whereas the general upper bound is quasipolynomial.','The source asks to improve the lower bound; the expanded quantitative target must apply to arbitrary translations over words, not only particular constructions or tree languages.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','previous','disambiguation'])
),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.12620','https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.09966'])
